Assume $\epsilon_j=0$ for some $1 < j < N$. Then : $$ F_N([\epsilon]_1^N) = F_{N-j+1}([\epsilon]_{j+1}^N) $$  egin{proof} 
oindent $F$ can be written as a sum of conditional entropies :  egin{equation} F_N = -\sum_{[Z]_1^N} P([Z]_1^{N-1}) P(Z_N | [Z]_1^{N-1}) \log P(Z_N | [Z]_1^{N-1}) \end{equation} 
oindent Where the dependence on $[\epsilon]_1^N$ and $M,T$ comes through the probabilities $P(..)$. Since $\epsilon_j=0$, we must have $X_j = Z_j$, and therefore (since the $X_i$'s form a Markov chain), conditioning further to the past is 'blocked', that is :  egin{equation} \epsilon_j = 0 \Rightarrow P(Z_N | [Z]_1^{N-1}) = P(Z_N | [Z]_j^{N-1}) \end{equation} 
oindent (Note that eq. (ef{blocking_cond}) is true for $j < N$, but not for $j=N$). Substituting in eq. (ef{F_cond_eq}) gives : $$ F_N = -\sum_{[Z]_1^N} P([Z]_1^{N-1}) P(Z_N | [Z]_j^{N-1}) \log P(Z_N | [Z]_j^{N-1}) = $$ $$ -\sum_{Z_{j}^N} P([Z]_j^{N-1}) P(Z_N | [Z]_j^{N-1}) \log P(Z_N | [Z]_j^{N-1}) $$  egin{equation} = F_{N-j+1} \end{equation} \end{proof}

